\title{Rcpp Style Guide}
\author{Ivan Jacob Agaloos Pesigan}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

This guide extends the R style guide to C++.
Where it is silent, the R guide applies, and then the Google C++ style guide
(\url{https://google.github.io/styleguide/cppguide.html}).
C++ keeps its \texttt{return}.
Agents follow \texttt{.setup/ai/style/rcpp.md},
which follows this guide and is changed together with it.

\section{Files}

\begin{itemize}
	\item Sources are in \texttt{.setup/cpp}, with \texttt{Makevars} and \texttt{Makevars.win}.
	\texttt{Build()} copies them to \texttt{src}; never edit \texttt{src} directly.
	\item Every project with C++ has \texttt{.setup/cpp/000-forward-declarations.cpp},
	which declares every function,
	so the order of the other files does not matter.
	It is the only numbered file.
	\item One function per file:
	\texttt{<pkg>-<name>.cpp} for a function exported to users,
	\texttt{<pkg>-<name>-dot.cpp} for an internal one.
	Numbered helper files (\texttt{001-transformation-helpers.cpp})
	are split into these.
	\item Each file starts with a header block whose \texttt{edit} line names the file itself.
\end{itemize}

\begin{Verbatim}
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// edit .setup/cpp/template-foo-dot.cpp
// Ivan Jacob Agaloos Pesigan
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\end{Verbatim}

\section{Types}

\begin{itemize}
	\item RcppArmadillo by default (\texttt{arma::mat}, \texttt{arma::vec}),
	with \texttt{// [[Rcpp::depends(RcppArmadillo)]]}.
	\item Plain Rcpp types only at the R boundary
	(\texttt{Rcpp::List}, \texttt{Rcpp::Named}).
	\item Matrices and vectors are \texttt{const} references unless the function changes them;
	scalars are passed by value.
\end{itemize}

\section{Names}

\begin{itemize}
	\item Functions in \texttt{BigCamelCase}; arguments and variables in \texttt{snake\_case}.
	\item Explicit namespaces: \texttt{arma::}, \texttt{Rcpp::}, \texttt{std::}.
	No \texttt{using namespace}.
	\item Every function has complete roxygen comments (\texttt{//'}), as in the R style guide.
	\item Internal functions are exported to R with a dot:
	\texttt{// [[Rcpp::export(.Foo)]]}, with \texttt{@noRd}.
	Public functions have \texttt{// [[Rcpp::export]]}.
	\item A helper library's C++ follows these rules, in \texttt{.setup/cpp/}.
	A project never edits or restyles a copy; it is changed only in its library.
\end{itemize}

\section{Bodies}

\begin{itemize}
	\item Every \texttt{if}, \texttt{else}, \texttt{for}, and \texttt{while} body is in braces.
	\texttt{make style} inserts them.
	\item Lines under 80 characters, including roxygen comments.
	\item Sanity checks go in the R wrapper (\texttt{.CheckFoo()}).
	\texttt{Rcpp::stop()} in C++ is only for numerical failures.
	\item \texttt{// \# nocov start} and \texttt{// \# nocov end} around unreachable branches only.
\end{itemize}

\begin{Verbatim}
// [[Rcpp::depends(RcppArmadillo)]]
#include <RcppArmadillo.h>

// [[Rcpp::export(.Scale)]]
arma::mat Scale(const arma::mat& x_mat, const double scale) {
  if (scale == 0.0) {
    Rcpp::stop("scale is zero.");
  }
  return x_mat / scale;
}
\end{Verbatim}

\section{Tests}

C++ functions are tested from R, as R functions, in \texttt{tests/testthat}.

\section{Tools}

\begin{itemize}
	\item \texttt{make style}: \texttt{clang-format} (Google style, \texttt{InsertBraces}).
	\item \texttt{make lint}: the header line, forward declarations, file names,
	names, \texttt{using namespace}, \texttt{const} references, and the format.
	Warnings in rProject 0.0.63; errors from 0.0.64.
\end{itemize}

\nocite{RCoreTeam-2026}

